occupationalSummary:
&lt;p&gt;This occupation is found in the the electrical supply industry. This industry is made up of mainly large employers who are responsible for distribution of electricity around the country, from where it is generated to where it is used. Through networks of overhead lines, underground cables and substations, ensuring that electricity is available where it is needed. Electrical power network design and planning engineers may work on the high voltage or the low voltage network for a network operator, system operator or a contractor, with different parts of the network having their own role, procedures and equipment. Design and planning engineers usually work in an office, designing and planning network extensions and optimisation schemes.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This is a core and options apprenticeship. An apprentice must be trained and assessed on the core and one option relevant to their role. The options are:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;•&lt;span style=&quot;white-space: pre&quot;&gt; &lt;/span&gt;design engineer&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;•&lt;span style=&quot;white-space: pre&quot;&gt; &lt;/span&gt;planning engineer&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The broad purpose of the occupation is to maintain a safe, secure and reliable network supply in normal and abnormal conditions. Design engineers design cost effective and policy compliant network alterations and asset refurbishments, for example new underground cable routes, substation refurbishments or overhead line extensions. This involves visiting sites and liaising with stakeholders to ensure that the plans they develop meet any site specific, health, safety and environmental requirements. They then produce concept feasibility and detailed drawings using design software and provide information to support colleagues with the purchase of equipment.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Planning engineers develop plans for the delivery of large-scale projects, asset health interventions, network maintenance and optimisation work to ensure the security of the network at all times. They liaise with key stakeholders such as customers, control engineers, the National Energy Systems Operator (NESO), project engineers and work teams to plan and communicate outage plans to ensure that the network operates reliably and effectively.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;In their daily work, an employee in this occupation interacts with a wide range of stakeholders such as distribution network operators, transmission network operator, contractors, planning departments, regulators and customers to plan or design maintenance, installation or optimisation projects. They must be able to maintain complex relationships through excellent communication skills. A electrical power network design and planning engineer typically reports to a senior engineer and works as part of a design or planning team. &lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;An employee in this occupation will be responsible for prioritising types of work taking into consideration regulatory requirements and resourcing or producing designs which are in line with design standards and meet the project brief.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
